Run long commands in the background

A cargo test takes three minutes. In the foreground that is three minutes of a
frozen turn — the user stares at a motionless prompt and nothing else happens.
run_command now takes background=true, and the command runs in its own process
while the conversation moves on.

The gate is the same one the foreground uses, and that is the point: exec.rs had
its "pass the gate" and "build the sandboxed command" halves pulled out into
pass_gate and spawn_sandboxed so both paths share them. Skipping approval in the
background would make approval decorative, since "put it in the background and
let it run" is the natural way around a prompt.

Ownership is a fence, not secrecy (deepseek-harness' rule): ids are predictable
(j1, j2), and only the session that started a job can read or stop it — another
session asking gets "no job called jN", which does not even confirm it exists.
The exec policy, sandbox and offline default are the same object the foreground
built, so a background command cannot be sandboxed differently.

Output goes into a fixed 64 KiB ring. Overflow drops the oldest bytes and the
read reports how many were dropped rather than failing: a chatty command should
not fill memory, and reading a slice of it should not be an error. Reads use
absolute cursors, wait=true waits at most 20s and then says it is still running
instead of letting the tool call time out. A finished job's remaining output is
written to <home>/outbox/jobs/ so it can be read after the process is gone —
in-process by design, since children are kill_on_drop and "keeps running across
a restart" is a supervisor's job, not this one.

Tests: 3 real-process tests (spawn, read, finish, land in the outbox; another
session refused; kill a running job) that skip without a sandbox, 2 ring tests
(overflow drops the oldest and says so; one huge write keeps the tail) and 2
evals. Docs: README tool rows, limitations, internals 6, CHANGELOG — EN + zh.
